<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jajce</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jajce is a town in central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bosnia">Bosnia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herzegovina">Herzegovina</a> at the falls where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pliva">Pliva</a> meets the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vrbas">Vrbas</a>. Its fortress was a seat of Bosnian kings, and the last king, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stephen-tomasevic">Stephen Tomašević</a>, was executed there in 1463. Retaken by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matthias-corvinus">Matthias Corvinus</a>, it held out as the centre of a Hungarian banate until 1527. In November 1943 the second session of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anti-fascist-council-for-the-national-liberation-of-yugoslavia">AVNOJ</a> met there and laid the foundations of federal <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yugoslavia">Yugoslavia</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jajce.jpg" alt="Jajce" width="320" height="400" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Golden Bosnian Lily · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jajce_(kola%C5%BE).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>At <b>Jajce</b>, from 21 to 29 November 1943, the AVNOJ's second session founded Democratic Federal Yugoslavia, a federation of six equal South Slav republics, named Tito prime minister and Marshal, and left the question of monarchy to a referendum.</blockquote><a href="/empire/socialist-federal-republic-of-yugoslavia">Socialist Federal Republic of Yugoslavia · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>According to Danon, at its first hearing, at the second session of AVNOJ in <b>Jajce</b> in 1943, where Tito was named Marshal of Yugoslavia, the audience spontaneously sang the line with Tito alone.</blockquote><a href="/songs/uz-marsala-tita">With Marshal Tito</a></li><li><blockquote>When Mehmed II conquered the Kingdom of Bosnia in 1463, King Matthias Corvinus of Hungary counter-attacked and retook the fortress town of <b>Jajce</b> after a siege in December 1463.</blockquote><a href="/state/banate-of-jajce">Banate of Jajce</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1463 Mehmed II invaded, captured Bobovac and executed the last king, Stephen Tomašević, at <b>Jajce</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-bosnia">Kingdom of Bosnia</a></li></ul></section>
